  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 402 PM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nToday-tonight... Upper level high pressure will remain in place as \nan upper level trough across the eastern US will continue to slowly \nshift south and eastward through the period. At the surface, high \npressure axis will shift southward into central Florida today. \nLocally, light south/southwest winds will shift onshore along the \ncoast this the afternoon with the formation of the east coast sea \nbreeze and more westward across the interior with the west coast sea \nbreeze. However, the east coast sea breeze is not expected to push \nas far inland today, given the deep offshore flow. The sea breeze \ncollision is forecast to occur a little closer to the coast than \nyesterday, generally around the I-95 corridor, later this \nafternoon/early evening. Much like yesterday, upper level shortwave \nenergy will aid in storm formation. \n\nScattered to numerous showers and storms are forecast to form this \nafternoon (PoP 50-70%). Isolated to scattered showers and storms are \ncurrently moving across the northern CWA from the west coast of \nFlorida this afternoon, with additional isolated showers forming \nalong the sea breeze in southern Treasure Coast. Highest coverage of \nstorms will be along the sea breeze collision, along the I-95 \ncorridor later this afternoon and into early evening. Storm motion \nwill generally be westerly today, which will bring activity back \ntowards the coast and offshore into the Atlantic. Forecast soundings \nshow a very moist profile, with PW values of 2.0-2.3\". The \nenvironment supports convection, with plenty of instability (MUCAPE \nof 1800-2300 J/kg), sufficient buoyancy (lifted index of -5 to -8), \nand sufficient downdraft potential (DCAPE 700-900 J/kg). However, \ntemperatures aloft remain rather warm (-5C to -6C at 500 mb). \nBecause of this, main storm hazards will be frequent lightning, \ngusty winds up to 45 mph, and torrential downpours. Quick amounts of \n2-4\" of rainfall  in a relatively short period of time in these slow-\nmoving storms will be possible, promoting minor/nuisance flooding in \nurban and poor drainage areas. The Weather Prediction Center (WPC) \nmaintains a Marginal (level 1 of 4) Risk for excessive rainfall for \nportions of east central Florida today, mainly from Cape Canaveral \nto Lake Kissimmee southward. \n\nTemperatures will be humid with seasonable to slightly above normal \ntemperatures once again. Afternoon highs will be in the low to mid \n90s with peak heat indices of 100-107F. Warm and muggy conditions \nwill continue overnight, with lows in the low to mid 70s. \n\nSunday-Friday... Upper level high pressure across the Great Plains \nwill shift eastward through mid next week as the trough over the \neastern US shifts eastward and offshore through early next week. At \nthe surface, and weak frontal boundary will drop into the Deep south \nand northern Florida by early next week. There is some discrepancy \nwith exactly how far this frontal boundary will make it before it \nwashes out by mid next week. Regardless, this will result in deep \nmoisture shifting over the area into at least Tuesday. Drier air \nwill begin to filter across the area mid-late week. Because of this, \nhigher coverage of showers and storms are forecast early next week, \nwith PoPs forecast to be 30-50% on Sunday, before increasing to 70-\n80% Monday, 70% areawide on Tuesday and Wednesday, and 40-70% \nThursday and Friday. Storm motion will generally be light and \noffshore. Storm hazards will be similar to what they are today. WPC \nhas outlooked portions of east central Florida in a Marginal (level \n1 of 4) Risk of Excessive Rainfall on Sunday and Monday. Seasonable \ntemperatures and humid conditions are forecast each day, with highs \nin the upper 80s to low 90s. Warm and muggy conditions will continue \novernight, with lows in the low to mid 70s.",
